  "id": "chaya_riojana_vidala_chayera",
  "name": "Chaya riojana (La Rioja carnival vidala chayera)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Carnival song of La Rioja province in north-western Argentina, sung at the February chaya, when people throw flour, water and basil at one another and the Pujllay, the carnival figure, is buried or burned at its close. The vidala chayera is sung by groups beating the caja, a small double-headed drum struck with a stick, often with guitar, in coplas whose lines are taken up and repeated by all; Riojan composers such as Ramón Navarro carried it onto stage and record. A generic membrane-drum card stands in for the caja.",
  "recipe": "classic modern chaya riojana, carnival Processional, andean branch. membrane drum pulse acoustic membrane drum, classical nylon-string guitar. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
